# 07_byovd_kernel_integrity.jky — kernel-module integrity and BYOVD triage.
#
# The problem this answers
# ------------------------
# "Bring Your Own Vulnerable Driver" is the technique that defeats endpoint
# protection from below: the attacker installs a driver the vendor already
# signed, then uses its IOCTL surface to read and write kernel memory, which is
# where EDR callbacks and token structures live. Nothing about that driver is
# malicious in isolation — it is a legitimate signed binary — so signature
# scanning cannot find it. What *is* anomalous is the combination: a module
# that is out-of-tree, unsigned, force-loaded, loaded long after boot, or still
# resident after its backing file was deleted, plus the global taint bits the
# kernel itself sets when any of that happens.
#
# This is the defensive half of SIH26148 pillar 3. JOCKY detects the technique
# rather than shipping it: `sys.taint()`, `sys.vulnerable_drivers()` and
# `det.byovd()` are read-only observations of `/proc` and `/sys`, and nothing
# here loads, unloads or modifies a module.
#
# Why the severities are graded the way they are
# ----------------------------------------------
# An in-tree module with a patched CVE (ip_tables, overlay, nf_tables) loads on
# ordinary hosts doing ordinary things. Grading those as high would put a
# permanent, unactionable item in every triage result — which is how operators
# are trained to ignore a detector. So `det.byovd()` grades them `info` with the
# CVE retained, and reserves `critical`/`high` for third-party drivers, which is
# the actual BYOVD shape. This script keeps that distinction and adds the
# correlation the grader cannot do for you.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Global kernel state: what the kernel says about itself
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `/proc/sys/kernel/tainted` is a bitfield the kernel maintains about its own
# integrity. Bits 12 and 13 are the ones that matter here: "out-of-tree module
# loaded" and "unsigned module loaded". They are cheap, kernel-authored, and
# cannot be hidden by a userland rootkit that is not already privileged.
#
# On Windows there is no such file, so `taint.value` is nil rather than 0. That
# distinction matters: `nil != 0` is *true*, so the obvious `taint.value != 0`
# reports a clean Windows host as tainted. The concept is absent, and the honest
# report is "not applicable here", not "false" and certainly not "true".
let taint = sys.taint()
let taint_known = taint.value != nil

emit {
  "kind": "kernel_taint_state",
  "severity": "info",
  "applicable": taint_known,
  "tainted": taint_known and taint.value != 0,
  "out_of_tree": taint.out_of_tree,
  "unsigned": taint.unsigned,
  "forced": taint.forced,
  "flags": taint.flags,
  "note": "bit 12 = out-of-tree module, bit 13 = unsigned module"
}

# A set taint bit is a fact about the running kernel, not a verdict: it is also
# set by staging drivers and by out-of-tree modules the administrator installed
# on purpose. It is recorded so the findings below have context.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. The graded findings
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `det.byovd()` runs the whole class in one pass over /proc/modules and
# /sys/module: known-vulnerable driver matched, out-of-tree, unsigned, forced,
# loaded late, backing file deleted, plus the taint summary. Each finding
# carries the module name in `evidence` so the next step can pivot on it.
let byovd_findings = det.byovd()
let flagged_modules = []
let worst = "info"

for f in byovd_findings {
  # `det.byovd()` speaks the runtime detector's shape (`check`, `title`,
  # `evidence`). It is re-emitted with a `kind` as well so that every finding this
  # library produces can be filtered by the same field, while the original
  # `check` and `title` are carried through untouched — the runtime's own view
  # stays intact and the two cannot drift apart.
  let evidence = f.get("evidence", {})
  emit {
    "kind": f.get("check", "byovd_finding"),
    "severity": f.severity,
    "check": f.get("check", ""),
    "title": f.get("title", ""),
    "evidence": evidence,
    "recommendation": f.get("recommendation", "")
  }
  if f.severity == "critical" or f.severity == "high" or f.severity == "medium" {
    # `f.evidence.module` is present on every per-module check; the taint
    # summary has none, and that is exactly the `get(default)` case.
    let module_name = f.evidence.get("module", nil)
    if module_name != nil and not contains(module_name, flagged_modules) {
      flagged_modules.push(module_name)
    }
  }
  if f.severity == "critical" { set worst = "critical" }
  elif f.severity == "high" and worst != "critical" { set worst = "high" }
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Two independent views of the loaded set, for corroboration
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `sys.modules()` is the raw kernel list; `sys.taint()` is the kernel's own
# summary. Reading both means a module that hides from one view still shows up
# in the other — the same principle as the hidden-module check, applied to the
# module table this script is reasoning about.
let modules = sys.modules()

# Only meaningful where the kernel publishes taint bits at all; on Windows the
# answer is "no such signal", which is different from "the signal disagrees".
let taint_agrees = nil
if taint_known {
  set taint_agrees = (len(flagged_modules) > 0) == (taint.value != 0)
}

emit {
  "kind": "module_inventory",
  "severity": "info",
  "count": len(modules),
  "flagged": flagged_modules,
  "worst_severity": worst,
  "taint_agrees": taint_agrees,
  "note": "taint_agrees false means a flagged module is not reflected in the kernel taint bits, or the reverse — worth a look either way; nil means this platform publishes no taint bits"
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. The check that actually decides the case
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A third-party driver that is *also* out-of-tree and *also* unsigned is the
# full BYOVD precondition, and no legitimate update path produces it. That
# combination is escalated here, because it is the only finding in this script
# an analyst should treat as hostile without further work.
#
# `sys.modules()` is the raw kernel list; the integrity verdicts (`out_of_tree`,
# `unsigned`, `forced`) come from per-module state under /sys/module/<name>/taint,
# which is what `sys.module_integrity()` adds. The raw list is used above for the
# inventory count; this loop needs the enriched view.
for m in sys.module_integrity() {
  if m.out_of_tree == true and m.unsigned == true {
    emit {
      "kind": "byovd_full_precondition",
      "severity": "critical",
      "module": m.name,
      "taint": m.taint,
      "path": m.sysfs_path,
      "recommendation": "out-of-tree and unsigned together is the full BYOVD precondition; hash the module, identify its origin, and treat the kernel as untrusted until the load is explained"
    }
  }
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. What an analyst does next
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The checks above answer "is the kernel in a state consistent with an ordinary
# update path". They cannot answer "who did it" — a module load is an event, and
# events live in timestamps. `byovd_late_loaded_module` carries the load time;
# correlating it with process and package activity is the pivot that turns an
# observation into attribution, and it is deliberately left to the analyst
# because the same timestamp is benign on a host that installs drivers on
# demand.
#
#   det.byovd()                      -> the graded findings
#   sys.vulnerable_drivers()         -> the curated reference list (20 entries)
#   sys.module_integrity()           -> per-module taint verdicts (out-of-tree,
#                                       unsigned, forced) for your own policy
#   sys.modules()                    -> the raw /proc/modules list
#   tl.merge(det.byovd(), ...)       -> timeline correlation with other sources
#
# Nothing in this file writes to the kernel. Loading a vulnerable driver to test
# a detector is the difference between studying a technique and deploying one.
